cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  gatingfactor: what actually gates distributed energy resource value
message: >-
  If you use this software or its findings, please cite it using the
  metadata below.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Morrissey
    given-names: Andrew
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0004-8758-2687"
repository-code: "https://github.com/ASM-21/gatingfactor"
url: "https://github.com/ASM-21/gatingfactor"
abstract: >-
  Four independent empirical studies of distributed energy resource value in
  PJM and the wider US grid, converging on a single structural finding: what
  gates a DER project is the context it sits in, not the sophistication of the
  control layered on top. Track 1 shows, over 36,441 Berkeley Lab Queued Up
  interconnection requests, that no cohort entering from 1995 through 2019 has
  had more than 34.1% of its requests reach operation, so there is no median
  wait for an entrant and the quoted time-to-interconnect median describes the
  completers; read before a cohort resolves, that median is also truncated
  1.55x to 2.06x low. Track 2 shows the FERC SGIP Fast Track
  15%-of-peak-load screen binds 5.7x to 17.1x below the voltage and thermal
  limit on synthetic distribution feeders modeled in pandapower. Track 3 shows
  that at equal revenue within customer class, the same rooftop solar customer
  is the best performer under time-of-use rates and the worst under a demand
  charge, a 69 percentage point swing. Track 6 shows that optimizing battery
  and EV dispatch against PJM's published average carbon intensity increases
  marginal emissions on 359 of 364 days, because the average and marginal
  signals are anti-correlated across the day.
keywords:
  - interconnection queue
  - hosting capacity
  - distributed energy resources
  - marginal emissions
  - carbon-aware scheduling
  - electricity tariff design
  - demand charge
  - time-of-use rates
  - PJM
  - power systems
  - survival analysis
  - pandapower
